===Etymology 1===
From {{compound|en|safety|valve|notext=1|type=exocentric}}.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=n|id=169691|date=March 2023}}</ref><ref>{{R:Dictionary.com|pos=n|source=Random House}}</ref>

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A [[relief valve]] [[set#Verb|set]] to [[open#Verb|open]] at a [[pressure#Noun|pressure]] below that at which a [[container]] [[hold#Verb|holding]] a [[gas#Noun|gas]] or [[vapor#Noun|vapour]] (such as a [[boiler]] or [[pressure cooker]]) would [[burst#Verb|burst]], thus [[reduce|reducing]] the pressure.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1941|month=April|title=British Locomotive Developments|journal=Railway Magazine|page=173|text=Spun glass mattresses are used for lagging the boiler, which has three Ross pop '''safety valves''' on the front ring.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1959|month=April|author=Cecil J. Allen|title=Locomotive Running Past and Present|journal=Trains Illustrated|page=185|text=[...] and to show that there was no shortage of steam, ''Royal Scot'', directly we stopped, blew off vigorously from its '''safety-valves''' steam that well might have been used in the cylinders in the interest of timekeeping.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} A [[valve#Noun|valve]] set to [[close#Verb|close]] a container holding a gas or vapour to [[prevent]] [[excessive]] [[loss#Noun|loss]] of pressure.
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} Any [[mechanism]] [[offer#Verb|offering]] [[relief#Noun|relief]] from [[physical#Adjective|physical]] or [[emotional]] pressure or [[tension#Noun|tension]].
#: {{synonyms|en|outlet|release}}
## {{lb|en|US|law}} A [[United States]] [[law#Noun|law]], [[codify|codified]] at 18&nbsp;USC&nbsp;§3553(f), [[authorize|authorizing]] a [[judge#Noun|judge]] to [[disregard#Verb|disregard]] [[mandatory#Adjective|mandatory]] [[minimum#Adjective|minimum]] [[sentence#Noun|sentences]] for some [[criminal#Noun|criminals]] with few or no [[prior#Adjective|prior]] [[offense]]s.
##* '''2020''' June 30, {{w|David Jeremiah Barron}} writing for the First Circuit Court of Appeals in case 19-1123:
##*: Hernández additionally requested that he be given a two-level '''safety-valve''' reduction pursuant to U.S.S.G. § 5C1.2, which mitigates the harsh effect of mandatory minimum sentences on certain first-time offenders who played only supporting roles in drug-trafficking schemes and who provided testimony about their involvement in the criminal activity.

